      "dom_id": "step-q181984",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following represents the set $S=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130949/assets/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,1],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130949/assets/494c9d77b0ff339b90792270c9d641054a03a2918e3c56a93747b42a77ee3ccf.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,1]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130949/assets/7c5ac5dad0aaacb4347c260d634e6bcd2d6102b25d51813fe106803417cc7539.png)",
